    "updated_at": "2022-08-25T22:47:02Z",
    "body": "Neither of these commands seem to solve the problem. I'm not using cargo install-update; the typical cargo install fails with the error messages about yanked dependencies that I posted above.\r\n\r\nCan you say more about how you're expecting those commands solve the problem?\r\n\r\nFor example, what I see (and correct me if I'm wrong) is there's a problem in a dependency that is beyond the starship codebase, except that the starship codebase doesn't exclude/prohibit/omit the broken dependency version. Is this generally what you're seeing too?\r\n\r\nIf so, then the problem would seem likely to affect other starship users beyond my local system. Yes? What I see is that the options of --enforce-lock and --locked apply only to my own local system. In other words, the options are a local workaround only, and do not fix the problem in the codebase for anyone else's system. Is this generally what you're seeing too?\r\n\r\nWhat I'm seeing is some way for the starship codebase to exclude/prohibit/omit the broken dependency version, and then to release an update. My understanding is that way the problem will be solved for everyone, and also solve it going forward, so long as the user doesn't do the --enforce-lock approach.",
